{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROW 12's AC NOW PRESCRIBES THE WRONG MECHANISM — flagged, not silently diverged from\n\nCriterion 11's oracle table specifies, for row 12 outbound:\n\n> sub-task case: `fields.parent`; EPIC case: NOT round-tripped — `pytest.raises(NotImplementedError)`\n> against the transport\n\nand the criterion defends that carve-out at length: routing it through the reconciler \"would make it\nuntestable by construction, because `dispatch_one._update_one_apply_parent` catches `Exception` and\nonly logs\".\n\n**That reasoning was sound and the prescription is now wrong**, for a reason outside this ticket:\n[rebar:39c1-2a32-b564-4b4b] set out to make the epic case WORK, and change **1311** implements it as\na write to the instance-discovered \"Epic Link\" custom field. An AC that requires the epic case to\nraise `NotImplementedError` would, if enforced literally, forbid the fix 39c1 exists to deliver.\n\nThe live cell has accordingly been rewritten from `test_set_parent_declines_the_epic_case_loudly` to\n`test_outbound_epic_parent_round_trips_via_the_epic_link` — same row, same intent (prove what\n`set_parent` does with an epic parent, and never let `fields.parent` be written where DC no-ops it),\ndifferent expected outcome.\n\n**Two things worth noting so this is not mistaken for amending away a gap:**\n\n * The carve-out's ORIGINAL justification still holds and is preserved. The new cell also asserts\n   **directly against the transport plus a raw-REST read-back**, NOT through a reconcile pass —\n   precisely because `dispatch_one` still swallows the exception, so a pass-level assertion would\n   still be untestable by construction. Only the expected outcome changed; the observability\n   argument the AC makes is unchanged and is the reason the cell is shaped the way it is.\n * Coverage went UP, not down. The old cell asserted an exception type. The new one seeds a real\n   Epic, performs the write, reads the field back over raw REST, then clears it and asserts the\n   field is null — a genuine round-trip where there was previously none.\n\n**Proposed amendment to row 12's outbound oracle** (recorded here for the operator rather than\napplied unilaterally, since 5200's criterion 11 has already been the subject of one ruling):\n\n    | 12 | set parent | `.parent_id` is the parent's local id | sub-task case: `fields.parent`;\n    |    |            |                                        | EPIC case: the instance-discovered\n    |    |            |                                        | \"Epic Link\" custom field, read back\n    |    |            |                                        | by RAW REST (no transport getter)\n\n**Whether the new cell passes is NOT yet known** — 1311 has not been through a harness run, and\n[rebar:1a9f-50c0-e7a5-4fda] gives independent reason to expect DC may accept-and-ignore parent\nwrites. If it fails live, that is a product finding for 39c1, not a reason to revert this AC change:\nthe coverage is correct either way."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785785120037603001,"uuid":"151728e9-5384-486c-8f27-01483457934e"}